# TypeScript Test Typing Standards

Apply in addition to [typescript-no-inference.mdc](./typescript-no-inference.mdc) for all test files.

## 1. Mock Types — Explicit Interfaces

Define interfaces for mocks and use typed helpers instead of `as any` or double assertions.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — any or double assertion
const mockPerformance = { now: jest.fn() } as any;
Object.defineProperty(global, 'performance', { value: mock as unknown as Performance });

// ✅ GOOD — typed mock interface + helper
interface MockPerformance {
  now: jest.Mock<() => number>;
  getEntriesByType: jest.Mock<() => PerformanceEntry[]>;
}
function toDomPerformance(mock: MockPerformance): Performance {
  return mock as Performance;
}
Object.defineProperty(global, 'performance', { value: toDomPerformance(mockPerformance) });
```

## 2. renderHook Results — Explicit Return Types

Type `renderHook` results and destructured values.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — inference
const { result } = renderHook(() => useMyHook());

// ✅ GOOD — explicit type
type UseMyHookReturn = ReturnType<typeof useMyHook>;
type RenderResult = RenderHookResult<UseMyHookReturn, void>;
const { result }: RenderResult = renderHook(() => useMyHook());
```

## 3. Global Object Mocking

Use `Record<string, unknown>` for `delete` and extended `globalThis` for assignments.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — any
(global as any).window = mockWindow;
delete (global as any).performance;

// ✅ GOOD — typed extensions
const g = global as typeof globalThis & { window?: Window; document?: Document; performance?: Performance };
g.window = mockWindow;
delete (global as Record<string, unknown>).performance;
```

## 4. Jest Mocks — Typed Module Mocks

Type mocked modules and their return values.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — untyped
const mockFetch = jest.fn();
(mockAnalytics.trackEvent as jest.Mock).mockReturnValue(undefined);

// ✅ GOOD — explicit types
const mockFetch: jest.Mock<Promise<Response>> = jest.fn();
(mockAnalytics.trackEvent as jest.Mock<void, [string, unknown]>).mockReturnValue(undefined);
```

## 5. Assertions — Typed expect Targets

Use explicit types for variables passed to `expect()`.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — inference
const snapshot = result.current.getSnapshot();

// ✅ GOOD — explicit type
const snapshot: CoreWebVitalsSummary = result.current.getSnapshot();
expect(snapshot.lcp).toBeDefined();
```

## 6. Console and Spy Types

Type `jest.SpyInstance` and `console` method spies.

```typescript
const consoleSpy: jest.SpyInstance<void, [string, ...unknown[]]> = jest.spyOn(console, 'warn');
```
